A **Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM)** is a density model formed by a {{c1::convex combination}} of $K$ Gaussian distributions:

{{c2::
$$p(x | \theta) = \sum _ {k=1}^K \pi _ k \mathcal{N}(x | \mu _ k, \Sigma _ k)$$
::
$$p(x | \theta) = ...$$
}}

In the M-step of the EM algorithm, the updated **mean** $\mu _ k^{new}$ for the $k$-th component is calculated as:

{{c1::
$$\mu _ k^{new} = \frac{\sum _ {n=1}^N r _ {nk} x _ n}{\sum _ {n=1}^N r _ {nk} }$$
::
$$\mu _ k^{new} = ...$$
}}

The update equation for the **mixture weights** $\pi _ k$ in the M-step is:

{{c1::
$$\pi _ k^{new} = \frac{N _ k}{N}$$
::
$$\pi _ k^{new} = ...$$
}}

The M-step update for the **covariance matrix** $\Sigma _ k$ of a GMM is:

{{c1::
$$\Sigma _ k^{new} = \frac{1}{N _ k} \sum _ {n=1}^N r _ {nk} (x _ n - \mu _ k)(x _ n - \mu _ k)^\top$$
::
$$\Sigma _ k^{new} = ...$$
}}
